    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p5-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find a linear function whose domain is $[1,2]$ and whose range is $[3,4]$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=x+2$, for $x\\in[1,2]$.",
          "worked": "For a positive gradient, the endpoints are $(1,3)$ and $(2,4)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{4-3}{2-1}\\\\m&=1\\end{align*}$$\nWrite $f(x)=x+c$. Since $f(1)=3$,\n$$\\begin{align*}1+c&=3\\\\c&=2\\\\f(x)&=x+2\\end{align*}$$\nOn $[1,2]$, this function increases from $3$ to $4$, so its range is $[3,4]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "In fact, there is a second possible rule. Can you find it? Hint: The linear function could have positive or negative gradient.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=5-x$, for $x\\in[1,2]$.",
          "worked": "For a negative gradient, the endpoints are $(1,4)$ and $(2,3)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{3-4}{2-1}\\\\m&=-1\\end{align*}$$\nWrite $f(x)=-x+c$. Since $f(1)=4$,\n$$\\begin{align*}-1+c&=4\\\\c&=5\\\\f(x)&=5-x\\end{align*}$$\nOn $[1,2]$, this function decreases from $4$ to $3$, so its range is $[3,4]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      }
    ]
  },
